<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rajyavardhana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 606</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rajyavardhana was a king of the Pushyabhuti dynasty of Thanesar and the elder brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>. On the death of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prabhakaravardhana">Prabhakaravardhana</a> around 605 he marched against the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, who had killed his brother-in-law, the Maukhari ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>, and defeated him, but around 606 he was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shashanka">Shashanka</a>, king of Gauda. His death brought Harsha to the throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajyavardhana.jpg" alt="Rajyavardhana" width="320" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dayal, Deen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sculptures_near_Teli_Mandir,_Gwalior_Fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rajyavardhana, also known as Rajya Vardhan, was the elder of the two sons of Prabhakarvardhana and his queen Yasomati. The couple also had a daughter, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajyashri">Rajyashri</a>, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grahavarman">Grahavarman</a>, a member of the Maukhari ruling family at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>. Prabhakarvardhana was the powerful ruler of the Thanesar region, although the exact dates of his rule are uncertain.</p><p>Prabhakarvardhana had expanded his territory by defeating rulers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, Gandhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a>, and he had resisted an invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huna-people">Huna people</a>. He died while his sons were away fighting the Hunas, and Rajyavardhana succeeded him on the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The marriage alliance between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grahavarman">Grahavarman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajyashri">Rajyashri</a> had drawn the two families so close that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shashanka">Shashanka</a>, the ruler of the Gauda kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, found it unacceptable. He allied with the Malava kingdom, and their forces appear to have made a successful surprise attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>, in which Grahavarman was killed and Rajyashri captured.</p><p>Rajyavardhana retaliated at the head of a cavalry force of 10,000, while the main army of infantry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> followed under his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>. His victory over the Malava ruler was won against an advance guard of the enemy. He died as he moved on to press an action at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> itself. Harsha succeeded him as ruler of Thanesar and vowed to avenge his brother's death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Contemporary information about Rajyavardhana is limited. He is mentioned by the Chinese traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harshacharita">Harshacharita</a>, a seventh-century work by the poet and bard Bāṇabhaṭṭa, but neither gives an impartial account, and they differ in substantive details. The military historian Kaushik Roy describes the Harshacharita as historical fiction with a factually correct foundation. The account that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shashanka">Shashanka</a> invited Rajyavardhana to a meeting with treachery in mind rests on these two writers alone, both of whom had reasons to write unfavourably of Shashanka.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rajyavardhana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Allied with the king of Malwa, he killed <b>Rajyavardhana</b> of the Pushyabhuti dynasty around 606, provoking a long war with Harsha.</blockquote><a href="/state/gauda-kingdom">Gauda Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death his elder son <b>Rajyavardhana</b> was murdered by Shashanka of the Gauda Kingdom, and in 606 his younger brother Harsha took the throne, also inheriting the kingdom of the Maukharis at Kannauj.</blockquote><a href="/state/pushyabhuti-dynasty">Pushyabhuti dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
